WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Should public money help pay for election campaigns? That is the question

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.660
behind California Proposition four. Campaign financing pays for things like

3
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.400
mailers, advertising, and campaign staff. Public financing means government

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.620
money helps cover those expenses. Today, California generally bans that for

5
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.840
state and local candidates. Some charter cities, including Los Angeles and

6
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.840
San Francisco, already have exceptions. Proposition four would let more

7
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.260
governments offer public financing. It does not automatically launch a

8
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.500
program or send money to candidates.

9
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:43.327
Imagine a first-time candidate planning how to reach voters. She has local

10
00:00:43.327 --> 00:00:48.667
supporters, but printing leaflets and running advertisements cost money. A

11
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:54.087
future city program might match small donations. Here is a made-up example:

12
00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:58.227
twenty dollars from a resident, plus forty dollars from the city, gives the

13
00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:03.587
campaign sixty dollars. Those numbers are illustrative. Proposition four

14
00:01:03.587 --> 00:01:08.307
does not choose a matching rate or promise this candidate any money. The aim

15
00:01:08.307 --> 00:01:10.707
is to help candidates reach more voters.

16
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.440
You might ask: who qualifies, and who decides? A future program would set

17
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.900
detailed eligibility rules and its budget. Candidates would have to

18
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.380
demonstrate broad support in their district and accept spending limits.

19
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.120
Public money could not pay their fines, legal defense, or personal campaign

20
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:36.120
loans. Funds reserved for education, transportation, or public safety could

21
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.580
not be used. But other public funds spent on campaigns still have an

22
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.360
opportunity cost. The state estimates a few hundred thousand dollars a year

23
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.220
for guidance. That is separate from future campaign programs, whose costs

24
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.020
depend on later decisions and could be significant.

25
00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:58.587
A voter favoring reform might say: I want candidates with community support

26
00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:03.727
to compete, even without wealthy backers. Supporters hope matching donations

27
00:02:03.727 --> 00:02:09.107
would make ordinary residents more important to fundraising. Voting yes

28
00:02:09.107 --> 00:02:14.067
gives governments that option, subject to rules. It does not guarantee less

29
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:14.887
donor influence.

30
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:20.780
Another voter might ask: should taxpayers pay for political campaigns? They

31
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.580
may dislike funding candidates they oppose, or prefer other uses for public

32
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.380
money. The measure leaves future program budgets and many details undecided.

33
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.320
Public financing also does not eliminate private political spending. A no

34
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.540
vote keeps the general ban and existing exceptions.

35
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:45.647
What do polls show? In early September, PPIC found forty-two percent yes,

36
00:02:45.647 --> 00:02:50.607
fifty-three percent no, and five percent unsure among likely voters. A later

37
00:02:50.607 --> 00:02:55.287
September Berkeley poll found thirty-five percent yes, thirty-five percent

38
00:02:55.287 --> 00:03:00.447
no, and thirty percent undecided. These are different surveys, not a clean

39
00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:02.127
trend or an election forecast.

40
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.840
The choice is whether to allow future public campaign financing programs,

41
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.120
with potential benefits and public costs. Visit our website for the fuller

42
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.300
explanation, sources, and official voter guide. Then make your own informed

43
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.140
choice.
